course: dictionary
lesson: 6202
title: Spotting a misrepresentation
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A misrepresentation is a false or twisted picture of something, so people believe what is not true. The word starts with mis-, which often means wrong or badly. The rest comes from represent, which means to show something or stand for it. So a misrepresentation shows something wrongly.
explain: «Strategic misrepresentation» means deliberate lying to win approval, for example hiding a project's real cost. The word deliberate is the clue. The person chose to do it on purpose, so that people would say yes.
explain: A misrepresentation can be a picture, a chart or a story, not just words. A graph with a stretched axis can make a small change look huge. Ask yourself: what is this picture trying to make me believe, and is that really true?
fact: Misrepresentation is a noun. You can have one misrepresentation or several misrepresentations.
fact: Misrepresentation means a false or twisted picture of something, so people believe what is not true.
fact: In law, misrepresentation means a false statement that tricks someone into a deal or a contract.
fact: A drawing, chart or story that shows something wrongly is also a misrepresentation.
fact: «A misrepresentation of the facts» means the facts have been twisted to give a false picture.
exercise: A chart has its axis stretched so a small rise looks huge. What is this? | A fair and honest chart | A piece of art only | *A misrepresentation of the numbers | A spelling mistake
exercise: Which word means a false or twisted picture that makes people believe what is not true? | Medicine | Mistletoe | Measurement | *Misrepresentation
exercise: «Strategic misrepresentation» means deliberate lying to win approval. What does deliberate mean here? | *On purpose, chosen | Slowly and carefully | Very loudly | By accident, with no thought
test: Hiding a project's real cost to win approval is an example of what? | A rainy day | *A misrepresentation | A happy surprise | A neat chart with clear labels
test: In law, what does misrepresentation mean? | *A false statement that tricks someone into a deal or contract | A fair and honest contract | A drawing of a cat | A polite greeting
exam: Ella says a stretched graph cannot be a misrepresentation because all the numbers are correct. What is the best reply? | The word means happy | *She is wrong, a stretched axis can still give a false picture | Graphs cannot show anything at all | She is right, correct numbers are always fair
